     1	# Task #11 — The gatekeeper
     2	
     3	**Status:** spec, pre-build. Design-gate with Sol before writing code.
     4	**Owner ask:** "Build the gatekeeper — the one service that holds merge, deploy and spend."
     5	
     6	## The one-sentence version
     7	
     8	Today, an agent that wants to merge code, deploy, or spend money simply does it. The
     9	gatekeeper is the single service that holds those powers instead — agents *ask*, it
    10	checks the record, it does exactly that one thing, and it refuses everything else.
    11	
    12	## Why the database alone isn't enough
    13	
    14	Sol's line, and it's the whole task: *"The database cannot be the jailer if the prisoner
    15	can rewrite the jail."*
    16	
    17	The schema guards are real and they work — but every agent process today opens the same
    18	SQLite file with full write access. An agent that wanted to ship unreviewed work does not
    19	need to defeat a trigger; it can insert its own approval, or write its own passing
    20	verdict, or record a zero cost. The guards stop *mistakes*. They do not stop *reach*.
    21	
    22	So #11 is not another guard. It is a **separation of powers**:
    23	
    24	| Who | May do |
    25	|---|---|
    26	| Agents | read the DB; write their own work and events; **ask** for irreversible things |
    27	| Gatekeeper | the only holder of merge / deploy / spend; the only writer of approvals and budget |
    28	
    29	## Where it is now (grounded in the code)
    30	
    31	- `harness/dispatch.py::ship()` sets status to `shipped`. Anything that imports dispatch
    32	  can call it. Guards decide, but the *call* is available to everyone.
    33	- `harness/db/jobs.py::record_usage()` — spend is recorded by whoever is spending. The
    34	  cap trigger refuses a breach, which is real, but the same process could write a 0.
    35	- `harness/gauntlet.py::_reserve_review_budget()` reserves before a call — the right
    36	  shape, in the wrong place. It should be *asking the gatekeeper*, not writing usage.
    37	- The `approvals` table already exists with guards (`guard_no_approval_is_born_granted`,
    38	  `guard_an_approval_is_used_once`, `guard_no_approval_without_readback`). **Nobody
    39	  grants approvals today.** That is the gatekeeper's job.
    40	- Migrations 001–006 harden the record. None of them constrain *who holds the pen*.
    41	
    42	## What to build
    43	
    44	1. **A separate process** on loopback (never the tailnet, never public), with its own
    45	   database connection. It is the only component whose sqlite handle is writable for
    46	   `approvals`, `usage`, and the `shipped` transition.
    47	2. **Three verbs, and nothing else.** `merge(job)`, `deploy(target)`, `spend(seat, cents)`.
    48	   No generic "run this". A gatekeeper that can be asked to run arbitrary commands is
    49	   just a shell with extra steps.
    50	3. **It checks the record itself** — it never trusts the asker's account of the record.
    51	   For `merge`: the panel passed, on the current version, with the family floor met, no
    52	   unresolved escalation. It re-reads all of that.
    53	4. **Reserve before spend.** `spend` is a reservation with a receipt; the provider call
    54	   happens after, and reconciles against the receipt.
    55	5. **Refuse loudly and legibly.** Every refusal is an event on the job in plain English,
    56	   because a silent refusal reads exactly like a thing that never got asked.
    57	6. **Deploys stay manual** (Decision D — Neill gates production deploys). The gatekeeper
    58	   *can* deploy; it will only do so on a recorded owner approval, tapped, not spoken.
    59	
    60	## The part that makes it real (and the part that will be argued)
    61	
    62	**Agents must lose write access to the safety tables.** Until that happens, the
    63	gatekeeper is a polite convention — a door next to a hole in the wall. SQLite has no
    64	per-table permissions, so this needs one of:
    65	
    66	- **(a) A second OS user** owning the DB file; agents connect through the gatekeeper's
    67	  socket for anything privileged and hold a read-only handle otherwise. Strongest.
    68	  Matches the arch doc's "credential absence via separate user" answer.
    69	- **(b) Split databases** — a `safety.db` only the gatekeeper can write, and a `work.db`
    70	  agents own. Weaker (an agent running as the same user can still open the file), but
    71	  far less machinery.
    72	
    73	Recommend **(a)**, staged: build the service and the three verbs first (useful
    74	immediately), then move the file ownership. Do not let the ownership move block the
    75	service — but do not call #11 done until it has happened, because without it the
    76	prisoner still has the pen.
    77	
    78	## Risks to pressure-test (for Sol)
    79	
    80	- **The gatekeeper becomes a shell.** Any drift toward a generic "do this for me" verb
    81	  destroys the whole point. What stops that drift?
    82	- **Trusting the asker.** Anywhere the gatekeeper takes a job id and believes something
    83	  the caller said about it is a hole.
    84	- **Confused deputy.** An agent asks the gatekeeper to merge job 7 when it was working on
    85	  job 9. How does the gatekeeper know who is asking, and about what?
    86	- **Availability.** If the gatekeeper is down, everything irreversible stops. That is the
    87	  correct failure — verify nothing falls back to the old direct path when it's unreachable
    88	  (that fallback would be the bypass).
    89	- **Prompt injection.** The agent is authorized to read the repo; a hostile file can tell
    90	  it to ask for a merge. The gatekeeper's answer must not depend on the agent's reasoning
    91	  at all — only on the record.
    92	- **The emergency stop (#13)** must be able to halt the gatekeeper, and must not itself
    93	  depend on the gatekeeper being alive.
    94	
    95	## Out of scope
    96	
    97	- Real phone-app auth (separate; "on the tailnet" is not proof of being Neill).
    98	- The Arch EMR repo — out of scope entirely (Decision C, PHI). Grok does not touch it
    99	  (owner decision, 2026-07-21).
   130	      *generates* a text argument — that's another model interpreting him, not a
   131	      transcript. Design around it.
   132	
   133	### Fix the record layer
   134	- [ ] **Tie reviews to a version.** Today: approve version A, builder changes it to B,
   135	      **the old approval still counts.** Sol calls this the most dangerous flaw — it
   136	      produces *"believable green checks on code nobody reviewed."*
   137	- [ ] **A fail must condemn a VERSION, not the job forever.** Right now a fixed build can
   138	      never ship.
   139	- [ ] **Block DELETING verdicts** (editing fail→pass is blocked; deleting isn't).
   140	- [ ] **Reserve budget BEFORE the provider call.** Refusing to *record* a charge doesn't
   141	      unspend the money.
   142	- [ ] **Agents get NO write access to the safety database.** *"The database cannot be the
   143	      jailer if the prisoner can rewrite the jail."*
   144	
   145	### The missing pieces
   146	- [ ] **A trusted gatekeeper service** holding all irreversible powers (merge, deploy,
   147	      spend). Agents *ask*; it checks the approval, does exactly that one thing, refuses
   148	      everything else. **This is what makes a database permission mean anything.**
   149	- [ ] **A hard emergency stop** that doesn't depend on Chief being alive.
   150	- [ ] **Separate cancellation** for: the speech, Chief's current thought, queued work, a
   151	      running builder. *("No, wait" currently stops the mouth but NOT the work.)*
   152	- [ ] **Numbered conversation turns** so an old answer can't arrive after a newer
   153	      correction.
   154	- [ ] **Duplicate protection** so a retry can't start the same job twice.
   155	- [ ] **Crash recovery** — reconcile jobs after sleep, power loss, network failure,
   156	      OpenClaw restart.
   157	- [ ] **Latency guard.** Chief spiked to 20–35s. A silent car is a failure — hard timeout
   158	      → *"I'm struggling, nothing's started."*
   159	- [ ] **A realistic driving test set** — road noise, passengers, radio, weak signal,
   160	      interruptions, self-corrections.
   161	- [ ] **Spec acceptance checklists, verified automatically.** When Chief specs a feature,
   162	      the system auto-writes a plain-English checklist of every feature and button that
   163	      spec promises, and where each one lives in the app — Neill never writes it himself.
   164	      Then the gauntlet (the review panel) and the driving tester (Forge) have to check the
   165	      *running app* against that checklist before anything is called done: is each thing

